<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumyks</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kumyks are a Turkic people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>, living mainly on the plain between the mountains and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. They formed the core of the Shamkhalate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarki">Tarki</a> and other principalities, and Kumyk served as a common language of trade across the region. Their lands came under Russian rule between the 18th and 19th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kumyks.jpg" alt="Kumyks" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photography of F. Petrov, 1860 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kumyk_architect_Abdul-Wahab_son_of_Mustafa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
